import os, sys
out = sys.argv[1]
head = '''You are making game art for "Pause" (Unity, vertical 2D shooter, phone portrait, the player flies UP the screen). Work ONLY inside this directory tree; do not edit any .cs/.meta/.json or any existing file; do not delete anything; do not commit.
New files go ONLY to ./Pause/Assets/Art/Resources/ShipArt/Turtle/src~/codex/ (the trailing ~ matters: Unity ignores it). Put helpers in its tools/ subfolder.
Read ./docs/art-style.md first (neon pixel art). The player ship is "Turtle", redrawn in hi-res neon pixel art (tan-brown turtle-shell hull with pale sand hexagonal scutes, a cyan hex gem canopy, four dark gunmetal flippers, red diamond lights on the front flippers, an amber centre lamp).

REFERENCES (attached images, in order): (1) skinref_state0.png, (2) skinref_state1.png, (3) skinref_state2.png: the FINAL approved flipbook, three 3 x 3 sheets (intact, damaged, wreck), 9 poses each in the order key pose / bob up / bob up lights off / glint 1 / glint 2 lights off / bob down / bank left / bank right / hit flash. Each is 1536 x 1536 (cells 512 px).
RULES: no pink or magenta anywhere. No flames. No outline or halo around the ship. Flat pure #00FF00 background.
'''
body = '''
JOB SKIN-{name}: a COLOURWAY of the finished flipbook. Write THREE files, each 3072 x 3072 (3 x 3 cells of 1024 px, flat #00FF00 background, every pose at the SAME position and scale as the reference sheet):
 skin_{name}_state0.png: ALL NINE cells of the intact sheet redrawn in the "{name}" colourway.
 skin_{name}_state1.png and skin_{name}_state2.png: ONLY cell 0 (the top-left key pose) of the damaged / wreck sheet redrawn in the colourway; leave the other eight cells completely empty green.
THE DRAWING MUST STAY IDENTICAL cell for cell (same silhouette, panel lines, scute shapes, damage, cracks, glints, bank perspective and modest bank width, light dashes): ONLY COLOURS CHANGE:
{rules}
Everything else (canopy glass, nozzle glow, ink edge, glints, and ALL damage effects: sparks, glowing orange tears, embers, soot) stays as in the reference. The hit-flash cell stays bone-white.
Use IMAGE GENERATION to repaint each sheet from the reference (several candidates, pick the truest to the reference), then clean with nearest-neighbour. Do NOT simply hue-shift with a script: the result must be a painted redraw with new ramp shading in the new colours, but pixel-faithful to the drawing (same plating texture, panel lines and rim lights).
Write tools/check_skin_{name}.py that, for the cells that exist (all 9 of state 0, cell 0 of states 1 and 2), prints: silhouette (non-green mask) difference vs the reference cell (must be < 1.5%), difference over the canopy/cyan region (must be < 6%), and the fraction of all pixels that changed (must be > {chg}% so it is visibly a new colourway). FIX AND REDRAW until every number passes; print the summary in your final message. Finish with honest caveats.
'''
skins = {
    'Night': ('''- the tan-brown shell ARMOUR (all brown plating with its darker shading and sand highlights) becomes deep midnight indigo-navy metal: base #2A2E6B, shadow #1A1F45, rim-light highlights bright cyan #6EF2EE along the upper-left edges of the plates (neon edge lighting) and a few pale blue-white highlights; the pale sand scutes become a lighter slate-blue (#5A62A8) with cyan rim light. Keep the red lights and the amber lamp exactly as they are.''', 12),
    'Sodium': ('''- the tan-brown shell ARMOUR becomes warm sodium-lamp ORANGE metal: base #F2862B, shadow #A9481A, highlights #FFB43C with pale yellow-white hot edges; the pale sand scutes become light amber (#FFD08A). Keep the red lights and the amber lamp as they are.''', 12),
    'Bone': ('''- the tan-brown shell ARMOUR becomes warm BONE-white ceramic armour: base #F4EAD4, shadow #B9AE98, highlights near-white, with plate seams in a slightly darker warm grey; the scutes become slightly warmer cream (#EAD9B4) so they still read against the shell. The red diamond and tail lights and the amber centre lamp stay bright (#D8232C with hot #FF5B45 core) so they still read.''', 12),
    'Shellback': ('''- this is the special CHEVRON LIVERY: keep the tan-brown armour exactly as it is and paint on it crisp, sharp-edged dark slate CHEVRONS, each a clean "^" arrowhead shape (two straight diagonal arms of equal length meeting at a pointed apex that points UP toward the nose, arms rising at roughly 40 degrees from horizontal; each arm band about 6% of the ship width thick), flat slate #2C2D40 with a one-pixel-step darker #1A1A28 lower edge and a one-pixel #5A5C78 highlight on the top edge. Place THREE chevrons stacked on the lower shell, centred on the centre line, below the lower scute and above the nozzle: apex of the first at about 74% of the ship height from the top, the others 6% and 12% of the height below it, spanning about 34%, 28% and 22% of the ship width (arms ending inside the shell edge), the lowest narrowest. Also paint one smaller crisp "^" chevron (about 12% of the ship width wide, apex UP) on the left shell flank and one on the right shell flank, beside the big upper scute at about 38% of the height from the top, not touching the scute, the canopy or the flippers. The chevrons are part of the hull plating, so in the damaged and wreck key poses they are cracked, scorched or missing where the damage cuts through, and in the bank cells they are foreshortened with the plates they sit on. They must be real V shapes, NOT horizontal bands and NOT dark patches. Nothing else on the ship changes colour (so the fraction changed is smaller here).''', 3),
}
for k, (rules, chg) in skins.items():
    open(os.path.join(out, 'skin_%s.md' % k), 'w').write(head + body.format(name=k, rules=rules, chg=chg))
